P3.7 SWD wire-layer findings, agent session 2026-04-30
========================================================

Exit criterion: pyocd reset -u 0123456789ab --target rp2040 halts
and resets the connected Pico DUT through the synthetic CMSIS-DAP-v2
over USB/IP, end to end.

Outcome: NOT REACHED. The DPIDR read still returns DAP_TRANSFER_ERROR.
The error mode shifted across the three iterations from "all zeros"
(line stuck low or no clocks) to "all ones" (line stuck high), which
strongly implicates SPI-peripheral half-duplex direction switching
on ESP32-S3, but the symptom did not converge to a clean fix.

Iterations
----------

I burned three rebuild+flash+test cycles. The high-level changes per
cycle and their on-wire effect:

Iteration 1 (commit candidate "P3.7 fix #1"):
  Code:
   - Added power.dut_usb.on() and power.vtarget.on() to boot.up() ahead
     of start_usbip().
   - Replaced stub SWJ_Sequence(count, data) (was always calling
     swd_line_reset(), discarding the host-supplied bit pattern) with
     a real implementation that pushes the requested bits through a
     new io/swd.h primitive swd_swj_send_bits().
   - Replaced stub SWD_Sequence(info, swdo, swdi) (was discarding
     output, zeroing input) with a real implementation that emits or
     captures bits per CMSIS-DAP sub-sequence info byte, using new
     primitives swd_seq_out_bits() / swd_seq_in_bits().
  On-wire effect: pyocd's dormant-to-SWD selection alert (136 bits),
  activation code (12 bits), line resets (51 bits each), and the
  TARGETSEL multi-drop write (DAP_SWD_Sequence cmd 0x1d for RP2040
  core 0) are now actually emitted on SWDIO. Previously they were
  collapsed to a 50-bit line reset only, which never wakes the
  dormant SWD-DP on the RP2040.
  Result: DPIDR read still 0x08; engine trace shows ack_raw=0x00.

Iteration 2 (commit candidate "P3.7 fix #2"):
  Code:
   - Added per-transfer ESP_LOGW instrumentation in swd_transfer
     (gated on a counter set via new MP entry dapprobe.set_swd_trace).
     Logs hdr / ack_raw / ack / data / parity bits.
   - Hooked DAP_SWJ_Clock command in dap_core_process to forward the
     host-requested clock to swd_set_clock_hz. The vendored DAP.c only
     stores the requested rate in DAP_Data.clock_delay, which is
     consumed by the bit-bang SW_DP.c that we replaced; our SPI2
     engine had no path to learn the requested rate, so it stayed at
     the swd_default_config init value (10 MHz) regardless of what
     pyocd asked for.
   - Increased swd_swj_send_bits to do single-shot transactions up to
     512 bits (the SPI FIFO size on ESP32-S3) so the dormant-wake
     selection alert is contiguous on the wire (the alert match
     state in the target probably resets on inter-transaction gaps).
  On-wire effect: SCLK now drops to 1 MHz when pyocd asks via
  DAP_SWJ_Clock. Engine trace confirms.
  Result: DPIDR read still 0x08; engine trace ack_raw flipped from
  0x00 to 0x1f. All five MISO sample bits are 1 instead of 0. This
  is a different failure mode: the line is now sampled high-stuck
  instead of low-stuck.

Iteration 3 (commit candidate "P3.7 fix #3"):
  Code:
   - Restructured swd_phase_send_header / swd_phase_recv_ack into a
     single combined swd_phase_header_ack() that drives 8 header bits
     through the SPI **command** phase and samples (3 + TrnAfterACK)
     ACK bits through the MISO phase, all in one SPI transaction.
     This is the windowsair-tools pattern for ESP32-S3, which is
     the only published reference that drives SWD via SPI on the S3.
     Rationale: SOC_SPI_HD_BOTH_INOUT_SUPPORTED is 0 on ESP32-S3, so
     a single half-duplex transaction with both usr_mosi=1 and
     usr_miso=1 is unsupported. Driving the header via the command
     phase sidesteps that restriction and gets the peripheral's
     native command->miso direction switch at the phase boundary.
  On-wire effect: header byte is now driven via SPI command phase;
  one transaction handles header + ACK with hardware-managed line
  flip.
  Result: DPIDR read still 0x08; engine trace ack_raw=0x07. All
  three ACK bits are 1.

Causes ruled in / ruled out
---------------------------

RULED IN: SWJ_Sequence / SWD_Sequence stubs were broken.
  pyocd's RP2040 multi-drop bring-up issues 6 SWJ_Sequences and 1
  SWD_Sequence before the first DAP_TransferBlock. The previous
  port/swd_glue.c stubs collapsed every SWJ to a fixed line-reset
  and zeroed every SWD_Sequence. This unconditionally fails the
  bring-up regardless of any other bug. Now fixed.

RULED IN: swd engine clock never honored DAP_SWJ_Clock.
  Vendored DAP.c stores the requested rate in DAP_Data.clock_delay,
  consumed only by the bit-bang SW_DP.c that we replaced. Our SPI
  engine was permanently at its init clock (10 MHz) which is faster
  than what pyocd actually asked for (1 MHz) and beyond what the
  RP2040 SWD interface is guaranteed at without buffering. Now
  hooked at dap_core_process so swd_set_clock_hz is called when
  ID_DAP_SWJ_Clock arrives.

RULED IN: ESP32-S3 SPI half-duplex cannot use both MOSI and MISO
in the same transaction. Confirmed via SOC_SPI_HD_BOTH_INOUT_SUPPORTED
being 0 in IDF (also called out in research/spi2-swd-benchmark.md).
  My iteration-2 attempt to issue a single transaction with
  usr_mosi=1, usr_miso=1, sio=1 was a dead end. Iteration-3
  command-phase trick is the correct ESP32-S3 path.

LIKELY-RULED-OUT: power-rail-not-on at SWD-init time.
  Adding power.dut_usb.on() + vtarget.on() to boot.up() did not
  change the symptom. Either the Pico is being powered from another
  rail (USB-OTG VBUS via the S3, USB direct from laptop, or 3v3
  off the carrier), or the rail-on signal is harmless given the
  dev-kit lacks the TPS2595 power-FET and the GPIO is just
  floating. Either way, this is now correct in code for rev1 PCB.

NOT RULED EITHER WAY: ESP32-S3 SPI MOSI release between transactions.
  When the engine issues a pure-MISO transaction (`usr_mosi=0,
  usr_miso=1`) following a pure-MOSI transaction, the trace shows
  the line being sampled as 1s (the value of the last MOSI bit).
  This suggests the IO_MUX FUN_OE for GPIO11 is not being released
  by the SPI peripheral between transactions, only DURING the
  active MISO sub-window of an active transaction.

  The iteration-3 command-phase approach should let the peripheral
  release the line at the command->miso boundary internally. The
  ack_raw=0x07 suggests it does NOT release in this case either,
  although the symptom could also be "line is genuinely high"
  (target idle high or floating with weak pull-up).

  Wire-level test (mpremote REPL, with engine detached and GPIO11
  reconfigured as plain GPIO):
    drive HIGH, read = 1     (expected)
    drive LOW,  read = 0     (expected, line is connected)
    release, no pull, read = 1 in some cases, 0 in others
    release, PULL_UP, read = 1
    release, PULL_DOWN, read = 0
  The line is genuinely floating when no driver is asserting it,
  with no external pull. So the 0x07 / 0x1f sample pattern is
  consistent with the SPI peripheral driving 1 (last MOSI bit value)
  rather than the Pico responding.

  This points to the SPI hardware needing additional configuration
  to release the pad between transactions, or to a different
  technique entirely (eg toggling user.sio per-transaction, the
  windowsair pattern that we did NOT replicate).

NOT RULED EITHER WAY: Pico SWDIO actually wired to GPIO11.
  The wire test above shows GPIO11 is genuinely floating when
  released, which is consistent with EITHER (a) Pico SWDIO
  connected and idle high-Z, OR (b) wire is unconnected. Cannot
  distinguish without a scope or by physically lifting the wire.

  Pi Debugprobe is also enumerated on the host (Bus 003 Dev 027).
  If the Debugprobe is also wired to the same Pico SWDIO line,
  bus contention is possible. Out of scope for this agent to
  rewire.

What landed
-----------

Code on this worktree branch (committed in three commits, see
git log):

src/c_modules/dapprobe/io/swd.h:
  - new public API: swd_swj_send_bits, swd_seq_out_bits,
    swd_seq_in_bits, swd_seq_idle, swd_set_trace.

src/c_modules/dapprobe/io/swd.c:
  - swd_phase_send_header / swd_phase_recv_ack collapsed into
    swd_phase_header_ack which uses the SPI command phase for the
    MOSI side (windowsair-tools pattern, ESP32-S3 specific).
  - swd_swj_send_bits does single-shot SPI transactions up to
    512 bits (the SPI FIFO) so the 128-bit dormant-wake selection
    alert hits the wire contiguously.
  - swd_set_trace + per-transfer ESP_LOGW instrumentation.

src/c_modules/dapprobe/port/swd_glue.c:
  - SWJ_Sequence forwards bytes to swd_swj_send_bits instead of
    collapsing to swd_line_reset.
  - SWD_Sequence dispatches sub-sequences to swd_seq_out_bits or
    swd_seq_in_bits based on the info byte's DIN flag.

src/c_modules/dapprobe/dap_core.c:
  - dap_core_process snoops DAP_SWJ_Clock and forwards the rate
    to swd_set_clock_hz.

src/c_modules/dapprobe/moddapprobe.c:
  - new dapprobe.set_swd_trace(N) MP entry point.

src/mpy/annealage_pod/boot.py:
  - boot.up() turns on power.dut_usb and power.vtarget rails
    before starting usbip + dapprobe.

What's left (next session)
--------------------------

The lever I did NOT pull is wire-level instrumentation (logic
analyser on GPIO10/11). With the SPI peripheral driving the line
during what should be a tristate window, a 'scope or LA capture
will instantly disambiguate "SPI HW not releasing MOSI" from
"line is fine, target not responding".

Recommended next steps:

1. Capture GPIO10 / GPIO11 with a logic analyser during one
   pyocd reset run. Specifically, check whether GPIO11 goes
   high-Z (line floats to whatever the target drives) during
   the ACK window after the 8-bit header, or stays at the last
   driven value. With the iteration-3 command-phase build
   currently flashed, set_swd_trace(20) gives a single ESP_LOGW
   line per transfer; sync the LA to that.

2. If the SPI peripheral is NOT releasing MOSI between
   transactions, the canonical ESP32-S3 fix is to toggle
   user.sio = 1 just before START_AND_WAIT and back to 0 after,
   per the windowsair-tools per-transaction pattern. This was
   not replicated in iteration-3 because I expected the init-time
   sio=1 to be persistent. It may not be on ESP32-S3.

3. If GPIO11 IS being released cleanly, then the line state during
   ACK is genuinely 1 because either:
     a. The Pico target isn't actually wired through (cable / dev
        kit perfboard issue);
     b. The Pi Debugprobe is contending on the same line;
     c. The dormant-wake / TARGETSEL sequences in iterations 1-3
        still don't match exactly what the RP2040 expects.
   Action: physically isolate the SWD wire and verify with a meter,
   then re-test.

4. The current swd_seq_in_bits / swd_seq_out_bits used by
   SWD_Sequence are pure-MISO and pure-MOSI transactions, which on
   iteration-3 we now believe do NOT auto-tristate the pad between
   them. That's the same SPI peripheral bug as the SWD_Transfer
   path. Same fix (per-transaction sio toggle, OR command-phase
   for output bits) needs to be applied if 2 above is right.

